1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,360
Rome wasn't built in a day, and it didn't

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,980
fall in one either.

3
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,556
So when did the Western Roman Empire really end?

4
00:00:08,633 --> 00:00:11,773
At its largest, under the emperor Trajan, the empire

5
00:00:11,773 --> 00:00:14,933
covered roughly five million square kilometres.

6
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:15,823
It looked permanent.

7
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,240
Then came half a century of chaos.

8
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,540
Between two hundred thirty five and two hundred eighty

9
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:24,495
four, more than twenty emperors rose and fell.

10
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:26,507
Power drifted east.

11
00:00:26,507 --> 00:00:30,367
Constantinople became a new capital in three hundred thirty.

12
00:00:30,367 --> 00:00:34,707
In three hundred seventy eight, Goths killed the emperor

13
00:00:34,707 --> 00:00:35,207
Valens at Adrianople.

14
00:00:35,207 --> 00:00:39,107
By three hundred ninety five, two brothers ruled two

15
00:00:39,107 --> 00:00:39,540
halves.

16
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,680
Then the blows came faster.

17
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,080
Rome was sacked fifteen years after the split, and

18
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:45,800
again after sixty.

19
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:49,133
After eighty one years, the West was gone.

20
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,420
In four hundred seventy six, the general Odoacer deposed

21
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:57,100
the young emperor Romulus Augustulus.

22
00:00:57,100 --> 00:00:58,904
He named no successor in the West.

23
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:02,047
But only half of Rome fell.

24
00:01:02,047 --> 00:01:04,747
The western half lasted eighty one years after the

25
00:01:04,747 --> 00:01:05,447
split.

26
00:01:05,447 --> 00:01:07,732
The eastern half lasted over a thousand.

27
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,060
So why did the West fall?

28
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:13,300
Historians follow several threads.

29
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:15,180
Constant civil war at the top.

30
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,020
Invaders who reached Rome itself.

31
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:18,613
And an empire split in two.

32
00:01:19,667 --> 00:01:23,527
In nineteen eighty four, the historian Alexander Demandt gathered

33
00:01:23,527 --> 00:01:25,127
the explanations people had offered.

34
00:01:25,127 --> 00:01:27,496
He counted two hundred ten.

35
00:01:28,567 --> 00:01:31,247
Edward Gibbon gave his own answer.

36
00:01:31,247 --> 00:01:34,867
Rome's decline, he wrote, was the natural and inevitable

37
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:36,225
effect of immoderate greatness.

38
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,360
Rome didn't fall in a day.

39
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:41,800
It came apart over centuries, and half of it

40
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:43,423
lasted another thousand years.
